  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Familiar Rack",
    "overview": "Everything is where a Hevy user's hand already goes. The layout is Hevy's on purpose: its set table, its page bar, its three tabs, its feed. The skin is OpenBite's (D123): Nunito, warm greys, round shapes, each exercise in its own card on a pure black page. A new screen is right when someone who trained with Hevy for years finds every control where they expect it, and someone who uses OpenBite knows the look.\n\nThe mood is dark, calm and dense. Rows stay compact, parts are split by hairlines, and there is no decoration. The numbers are the content. Cards are black with a hairline drawn inside, not grey slabs, so the page stays one black surface and the cards read as outlines on it. Buttons and chips are full pills; set values sit in small rounded wells; ticks are circles.\n\nIt is not upstream openGym's iOS look: no large titles, no tinted icon tiles, no Apple system colours. It is not a gamified fitness app either: no gradients, no glow, no badges, no confetti, no hero numbers for show. Control sizes are Hevy's; D123 changed the skin, not the hit targets.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A pure black page. Exercises, feed workouts, routines and the Today row each sit in a black card with a hairline; Home's widgets and Settings run flat between hairlines.",
      "One accent, which the user chooses. It means \"tap this\", \"this is next\" and \"this is done\".",
      "The other colours carry meanings (warm-up, failure, drop set, superset, cardio, deload, record, note), and each steps aside when the accent or another meaning comes too close.",
      "Warm greys and warm white text, never cool blue-greys.",
      "Tabular numbers everywhere, so a column of sets lines up.",
      "One page bar on every screen: a bare chevron, a small centred title, plain actions."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "The accent is the only colour that means \"tap\", \"next\" or \"done\". A thing that is none of those never wears it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Step Aside Rule",
